      },
      "worked": "For any $x$ in the domain of $f$, $1+bx\\ne0$. Also, $ab\\ne-1$, so $1+ab\\ne0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1+bf(x)&=1+b\\frac{a-x}{1+bx}\\\\1+bf(x)&=\\frac{1+bx+b(a-x)}{1+bx}\\\\1+bf(x)&=\\frac{1+ab}{1+bx}\\ne0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $f(x)$ is also in the domain of $f$, so the second application is defined.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}f(f(x))&=f\\left(\\frac{a-x}{1+bx}\\right)\\\\f(f(x))&=\\frac{a-\\frac{a-x}{1+bx}}{1+b\\frac{a-x}{1+bx}}\\\\f(f(x))&=\\frac{a(1+bx)-(a-x)}{(1+bx)+b(a-x)}\\\\f(f(x))&=\\frac{(1+ab)x}{1+ab}\\\\f(f(x))&=x.\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore $f(f(x))=x$ for every $x$ in the domain of $f$. This includes $b=0$, when $f(x)=a-x$ has domain $\\mathbb{R}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
